Title: Last Wash
Credit: Written by
Author: An OpenAct sample script
Draft date: First draft

EXT. HIGH STREET - NIGHT

Rain hammers a row of shuttered shops. One window still glows: SPIN CYCLE LAUNDERETTE, the second N of the sign long dead, a bus shelter opposite with nobody waiting in it.

INT. SPIN CYCLE LAUNDERETTE - CONTINUOUS

Twelve machines, eleven of them silent. NELL (60s, cardigan, reading glasses on a chain) folds towels into perfect squares and stacks them by colour.

The door bangs open. KASIA (20s, soaked through) staggers in with a bin bag of laundry over one shoulder.

KASIA
Are you still open?

NELL
(without looking up)
Sign says ten. Clock says nine fifty-two. You've got eight minutes and a very full bag.

Kasia swings the bag onto the counter. It splits. Socks avalanche across the floor.

KASIA
I can explain.

NELL
You don't need to explain socks, love. Everyone's got socks.

Nell comes round the counter, kneels, and starts pairing them without being asked.

KASIA
You don't have to do that.

NELL
I know.

She holds up a sock with a hole in the heel, tuts, and tucks it into her cardigan pocket.

NELL
My husband used to say you can tell everything about a person from their washing. Wrong, as usual. You can tell everything about a person from what they do when the bag splits. Most people swear. Some people cry. One fella in nineteen ninety-eight walked out and left the lot, and I wore his jumper for twenty years because it was better than anything I owned. You said you can explain. Nobody has ever wanted to explain their socks before.

KASIA
(beat)
They're not mine.

Nell stops. Holds up a tiny pink sock. Then a man's black work sock. Then a sock with a cartoon fox on it.

NELL
Then whose are they?

KASIA
That's the bit I can explain.

SMASH CUT TO:

INT. SPIN CYCLE LAUNDERETTE - LATER

Machine seven churns. Nell and Kasia sit side by side on the plastic bench, watching it like television.

KASIA
Three flats on my landing. Nobody talks to anybody. But they all leave their washing outside my door, because I'm the one who comes here.

NELL
And they pay you?

KASIA
They say thank you. Mostly.

The machine shudders into its spin. Nell gets up, turns the sign on the door to CLOSED, and sits back down.

NELL
Right. Tell me about the fox.

> FADE OUT.

